# See https://docs.github.com/get-started/getting-started-with-git/ignoring-files for more about ignoring files.

# Maven build output
target/
*.class

# IDEs and editors
.idea/
*.iml
*.ipr
*.iws
.vscode/*
!.vscode/settings.json
!.vscode/tasks.json
!.vscode/launch.json
!.vscode/extensions.json
.project
.classpath
.settings/
.factorypath

# Logs
*.log
logs/

# System files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Local environment overrides — application-local.properties itself IS committed on purpose
# (see its own header comment: every value in it is a fake, dev-only placeholder). This is only
# for a personal override file layered on top of it, e.g. if you point at your own Atlas dev
# cluster instead of local MongoDB — never commit real secrets, even dev ones, in a file matching
# this pattern.
application-local-override.properties
.env
.env.local

# Real secrets should never live in this repo at all — this pattern is a safety net in case one
# ever gets created locally by mistake, not an expectation that it will be.
*.pem
*.key

# Review finding ("P0 #2" — investigating the admin-secret leak turned up a stale, vulnerable
# compiled Angular bundle committed here, weeks out of date with the actual source): the Angular
# production build is a generated artifact, never something to commit — the Dockerfile now
# builds it fresh every time. A stale committed build can silently ship old bugs (including
# actual security leaks) regardless of how many times the real source gets fixed afterward.
src/main/resources/static/*
!src/main/resources/static/.gitkeep
